From 9c751b4b2873a330bb74623bf19bb704b5461b18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Canar <davidcanar@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:54:40 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] TP: decode all-reduces over the OdinLink stream API
 (odl_ar2)

Cross-host decode collectives are tiny (5120 x rows fp32) and latency-bound.
RCCL over the ODL_TB5 net plugin needs ~88 us per 10 KB all-reduce on an idle
link plus a host stream sync after every op, so a decode token paid 80 host
round trips. With EXL3_TP_ODL_AR2=1 the backend routes all-reduces whose wire
payload fits the driver's 64 KiB latency path (EXL3_TP_ODL_AR2_MAX) through
libodl_ar2.so instead: a stream-ordered [stage -> doorbell -> GPU wait+add]
sequence with a CPU progress thread moving the bytes over OdinLink streams.
~34 us per 10 KB op, ~97 us per 60 KB op (6-row verify), and no host sync,
so the host queues the following layers while the exchange is in flight.

fp32 tensors travel as bf16 and come back exactly as the RCCL path rounds
them (fp32 -> bf16, bf16 sum with fp32 accumulation, -> fp32); both ranks
add src + peer, so the reduced tensors stay bit-identical across ranks
(the replicated MoE routing relies on it). Greedy outputs are unchanged.
Larger collectives (prefill) stay on RCCL.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
